3 | fn trim(s: str[8]) -> str[8] {
  |    ^^^^
  = hint: the standard library has trim(s) -> str[N]; call it, or choose a different name, e.g. my_trim

error[E0216]: split() takes its type, and so its capacity, from the variable it is stored in
  --> tests/errors/stdlib_misuse.al:10:11
   |
10 |     print(split(s, ","));
   |           ^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = hint: make the call the whole right-hand side of a typed let/var, an assignment or a return: let parts: list[str[32]; 16] = split(s, ","); or, to go through the pieces one at a time without a list: for p in split(s, ",") { }

error[E0201]: split() builds a list[str[K]; M], but str[8] is expected
  --> tests/errors/stdlib_misuse.al:11:21
   |
